WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.170
It also became
clear that there was

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:07.030
one other way to increase the
capacity of a communication

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:08.240
system.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.520
We can increase the number of
different signalling events.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.680
For example, with Alice and
Bob's string communication

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.530
system, they soon found that
varying the type of plucks

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.580
allowed them to send
their messages faster.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:29.630
For example, hard, medium,
versus soft plucks,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.740
or high pitch versus
low pitch plucks

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.620
by tightening the cable
different amounts.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.620
And this was an idea implemented
by Thomas Edison, which

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.540
he applied to the
Morse code system.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.300
And it was based on
the idea that you

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.550
could use weak and
strong batteries

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.210
to produce signals of
different strengths.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.350
He also used two directions,
as Gauss and Weber did, forward

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.160
versus reverse current,
and two intensities.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.200
So he had plus 3 volts,
plus 1 volt, minus 1 volt,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.420
and minus 3 volt-- four
different current values

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.830
which could be exchanged.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.630
It enabled Western
Union to save money

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.580
by greatly increasing the number
of messages the company could

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.300
send without building new lines.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.670
And this is known as the
quadruplex telegraph,

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.220
and it continued to be
used into the 20th century.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.010
But again, as we
expanded the number

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.310
of different signaling events,
we ran into another problem.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.520
For example, why not send
a thousand or a million

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.230
different voltage
levels per pulse?

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.950
Well, as you may expect,
fine-grain differences

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.746
lead to difficulties
on the receiving end.

00:01:43.746 --> 00:01:47.950
And with electrical systems, the
resolution of these differences

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.100
is always limited
by electrical noise.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:57.950
If we attach a probe
to any electrical line

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:03.520
and zoom in closely
enough, we will always

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.550
find minute, undesired currents.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.780
And this is an unavoidable
result of natural processes,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.630
such as heat, or
geomagnetic storms,

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.440
and even latent effects
of the big bang.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.090
So the differences between
signaling events must be great

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.000
enough that noise cannot
randomly bump a signaling event

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.320
from one type to another.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.800
Clearly, now, we can
step back and begin

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.390
to define the capacity of a
communication system using

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.390
these two very simple ideas.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.750
First, how many symbol
transfers per second,

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.040
which we called symbol rate?

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.590
And today, it's known simply
as baud, for Emile Baudot.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.790
And we can define
this as n, where

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.030
it's n symbol
transfers per second.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:58.400
And number two, how many
differences per symbol, which

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.130
we can think of as
the symbol space?

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:04.280
How many symbols can we
select from at each point?

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.930
And we can call this s.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.320
And as we've seen
before, these parameters

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.890
can be thought of as a
decision tree of possibilities,

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.640
because each symbol can be
thought of as a decision where

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.430
the number of branches depend
on the number of differences.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.030
And after n symbols,
we have a tree with s

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.020
to the power of n leaves.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:33.590
And since each path through this
tree can represent a message,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.560
we can think of the
number of leaves

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.460
as the size of
the message space.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.000
And this is easy to visualize.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.820
The message space is simply
the width of the base of one

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.730
of these trees.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.660
And it defines the total
number of possible messages

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:54.850
one could send given a
sequence of n symbols.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.740
For example, let's
say Alice sends Bob

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.330
a message which
consists of two plucks,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.360
and they're using a
hard versus soft pluck

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.920
as their communication system.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.360
This means she has
the ability to define

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.900
one of four possible
messages to Bob.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.430
And if instead they were using
a system of hard versus medium

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.149
versus soft plucks,
then with two plucks,

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:24.190
she has the ability to define
one of 3 to the power of 2

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.650
equals 9 messages.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:32.540
And with three plucks, this
jumps to one of 27 messages.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.980
Now if instead, Alice and Bob
were exchanging written notes

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.800
in class, which contain only
two letters on a piece of paper,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.050
then a single note would contain
1 of 26 to the power of 2,

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.825
or 676 possible messages.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.490
It's important to realize
now that we no longer care

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:58.750
about the meaning applied to
these chains of differences--

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.610
merely how many different
messages are possible.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.020
The resulting sequences could
represent numbers, names,

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.110
feelings, music, or perhaps
even some alien alphabet

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.800
we could never understand.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.320
When we look at a
communication system now,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.930
we can begin to think
about its capacity

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.230
as how many different
things you could say.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.880
And we can then use
the message space

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.140
to define exactly
how many differences

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.870
are possible in any situation.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:32.660
And this simple yet elegant
idea forms the basis

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.620
for how information
will be later defined.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:41.330
And this is the final step that
brings us to modern information

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:45.950
theory, and it emerges in
the early 20th century.

